 is that the final output distribution can be identical to ordinary autoregressive decoding from the target model. For each draft token, the method compares the target probability with the draft probability. If the target token is sufficiently likely, it is accepted. If not, the token is rejected and a new token is sampled from a corrected distribution that favors the target model\u2019s probabilities while accounting for the draft\u2019s proposal. This rejection-sampling step ensures that accepted tokens and the final replacement token are drawn from the same distribution the target model would have produced without speculation.\n\nIn practice, speedup depends on how many draft tokens are accepted before a rejection, the relative cost of the draft model versus the target model, and the efficiency of the target\u2019s parallel verification pass. If the draft model is too small or poorly aligned, it proposes tokens the target rejects often, wasting computation. If the draft is too large or the batch size is high, the extra draft cost and memory overhead can erode gains. The two most common reasons the speedup is smaller than hoped are low draft acceptance rates and high overhead from running the draft model or managing verification, especially under large batch sizes or when the target model is already compute-bound.\"\n\nCount words. Let's count:\nSpeculative1 decoding2 speeds3 up4 large5 language6 model7 inference8 by9 using10 a11 smaller12 faster13 draft14 model15 to16 propose1", "top2": [[[1596, -0.0018879104172810912], [22916, -6.751292705535889]], [[1144, -9.655998837843072e-06], [4087, -12.30902099609375]], [[4087, -0.22334875166416168], [310, -1.6189690828323364]], [[1156, -0.002030824078246951], [303, -6.274072170257568]], [[579, -0.25154057145118713], [25, -1.7026475667953491]], [[1622, -0.009637754410505295], [3296, -4.876171112060547]], [[25, -0.30982357263565063], [13, -1.3413519859313965]], [[59987, -0.14849352836608887], [7860, -2.3285319805145264]], [